      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To determine the weight of one bowling ball, we start by defining the variables and setting up the equations based on the given information.\n\nLet:\n- \\( B \\) be the weight of one bowling ball.\n- \\( C \\) be the weight of one canoe.\n\nFrom the problem, we know two things:\n1. Seven bowling balls weigh the same as three canoes.\n2. Two canoes weigh a total of 56 pounds.\n\nFirst, we express the weight of the canoes in terms of \\( C \\):\n\\[\n2C = 56\n\\]\nSolving for \\( C \\):\n\\[\nC = \\frac{56}{2} = 28\n\\]\nSo, one canoe weighs 28 pounds.\n\nNext, we use the first piece of information to find the weight of one bowling ball. We know that seven bowling balls weigh the same as three canoes. Therefore:\n\\[\n7B = 3C\n\\]\nSubstituting the value of \\( C \\) (which is 28 pounds) into the equation:\n\\[\n7B = 3 \\times 28\n\\]\n\\[\n7B = 84\n\\]\nSolving for \\( B \\):\n\\[\nB = \\frac{84}{7} = 12\n\\]\nSo, one bowling ball weighs \\(\\boxed{12}\\) pounds.",
