    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "We start with the information that two canoes weigh a total of 56 pounds. This can be written as:\n\n\\[ 2 \\text{ canoes} = 56 \\text{ pounds} \\]\n\nNext, we need to find the weight of one canoe. We do this by dividing the total weight of two canoes by 2:\n\n\\[ \\text{Weight of one canoe} = \\frac{56 \\text{ pounds}}{2} = 28 \\text{ pounds} \\]\n\nNow, we know that three identical canoes weigh the same as seven identical bowling balls. Let's denote the weight of one canoe by \\( C \\) and the weight of one bowling ball by \\( B \\). From the problem, we have:\n\n\\[ 3C = 7B \\]\n\nWe already found that \\( C = 28 \\) pounds, so we substitute this value into the equation:\n\n\\[ 3 \\times 28 = 7B \\]\n\nSimplifying the left side of the equation, we get:\n\n\\[ 84 = 7B \\]\n\nTo find the weight of one bowling ball, we solve for \\( B \\) by dividing both sides of the equation by 7:\n\n\\[ B = \\frac{84}{7} = 12 \\]\n\nTherefore, the weight of one bowling ball is \\(\\boxed{12}\\) pounds.",
